---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-lactation`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Lactation / Breastfeeding',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Lactation', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Lactation: Why Standard Doses Don't Reach Breastmilk (And What Does)"
  description="Why standard 400-600 IU maternal vitamin D doesn't meet the breastfed infant's requirement, the Wagner-Hollis 6,400 IU protocol, and infant supplementation alternatives."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-lactation", label: "Lactation" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Lactation / Breastfeeding</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Breastmilk is nutritionally superior for infants in almost every respect —
      but historically low in one nutrient: vitamin D. A mother with normal
      25(OH)D supplies only ~25-50 IU/L in breastmilk, well below the infant
      RDA of 400 IU. This is why AAP recommends infant supplementation from
      birth. High-dose maternal supplementation is a validated alternative.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The transfer physiology</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Vitamin D crosses into breastmilk in proportion to maternal serum
      25(OH)D and, more responsively, to recent oral intake. Standard
      supplementation (400-1,000 IU/day) produces very modest breastmilk
      concentrations because the mother's tissues absorb the vitamin D
      before it reaches breastmilk in sufficient quantity.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Wagner-Hollis 6,400 IU trial</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Wagner & Hollis 2015 (Pediatrics) randomised breastfeeding mothers to
      400, 2,400, or 6,400 IU/day cholecalciferol. Infants received either
      400 IU/day drops or nothing depending on maternal arm. Result:
      infants of mothers taking 6,400 IU/day achieved the same 25(OH)D as
      infants directly supplemented at 400 IU/day. No infant hypercalcaemia
      or adverse events.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Two valid approaches</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Infant drops</strong> — 400 IU/day cholecalciferol directly to the breastfed baby. Standard AAP recommendation. Products: D-Vi-Sol, Baby Ddrops, Enfamil D.</li>
      <li><strong>Maternal 6,400 IU/day</strong> — mother takes 6,400 IU/day; infant needs no drops. Compliance often better; single-actor solution.</li>
      <li>Choose based on family preference and compliance factors.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Lactation RDA</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>IOM RDA for lactating women: 600 IU/day (same as pregnancy).</li>
      <li>Practical clinical dosing often 1,000-2,000 IU/day for mother, plus 400 IU/day infant drops.</li>
      <li>Wagner-Hollis maternal option: 6,400 IU/day mother, no infant drops.</li>
      <li>Upper limit 4,000 IU/day officially, though 6,400 IU/day in the trial was safe.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical timing</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Take the daily maternal dose consistently — the transfer into breastmilk
      is dose-responsive over hours to days. Missing days significantly
      reduces breastmilk vitamin D. Time of day doesn't matter much; with a
      meal for fat co-absorption is best.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Either dosing approach (infant
      drops or high-dose maternal) is safe and effective. See our
      <a href="/vitamin-d-for-infants" class="text-amber-700 underline">infants page</a>
      for the full picture.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-lactation" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
